WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:06.900
Just one pint of donated blood is enough to
save three lives.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.660
The blood you donate is actually sold on the
open market and the blood industry is worth

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.219
$4.5 billion per year.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:15.959
But blood saves lives.

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.760
If you began donating blood at the age of
17 and donated every 56 days, until you reach

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.620
76 years old.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.810
You will have saved more than 1,000 lives.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.770
But not everyone’s blood is the same.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:34.220
Hollywood movies and TV shows regularly remind
us that before someone can undergo a blood

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.950
transfusion, medical professionals must find
out that person’s blood type and match it

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.450
with a suitable donor’s blood type.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:42.630
But why?

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.239
What would happen if you were given the wrong
blood type?

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:48.080
What even is a blood type and why do we have
them to begin with?

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.180
Let’s find out.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:01:00.719
There are four major blood types: A, B, AB
and O. Blood type is hereditary and which

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.430
one you have, depends on your parents.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:09.210
If both parents have A blood type, the child
will most likely have an A blood type as well.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:14.299
Similarly, B and B-type parents will produce
offspring with B-type blood.

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:20.230
However, if one parent is A-type and the other
B-type, the child will be AB-type.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:26.479
Okay that’s all quite simple, but things
get trickier when we introduce the O blood

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:27.479
type.

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:32.000
A and B blood types are dominant genes, or
“alleles” to be more precise.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.150
Whereas the allele responsible for O blood
type in recessive.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:41.799
This means if one parent has an O blood type
and the other parent has either an A or B

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:47.820
blood type, then the child is most likely
to have either an A or B blood type.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.090
In order for someone to have an O blood type,
both their parents have to have O blood types

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.090
too.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.880
Making O blood types extremely rare and highly
sought after in the medical field, there’s

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.950
also another reason why donors with an O blood
type are desired so much, but we’ll get

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:03.950
to that later.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.409
It’s all very well labelling them with letters
but what does your blood type actually say

00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:11.039
about your blood?

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.670
What makes A blood different from B blood?

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:21.340
Well, it all has to do with what type of sugars
and proteins sit on the red blood cell.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.780
Antigens are proteins or sugars that attach
themselves to the membrane of red blood cells.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.190
An antigen acts as an identifier to your immune
system.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:35.510
There are many different varieties of antigens
and each has their own unique signature, a

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:38.450
way of identifying itself to your own immune
system.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.950
I’m sure you’ve heard of antibodies, they’re
the little armoured knights of our bodies.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.250
Whenever a foreign object, enters our blood
stream, the immune system instantly dispatches

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.010
antibodies, like flying ninja-monkeys.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:56.250
They make their way around the body and attach
themselves to cells, if they find a cell that

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:02.210
wasn’t created by our own body, such as
an evil pathogen, then the antibody exterminates

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.210
it.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:09.440
But how does the antibody know which cells
belong to our body and which are foreign intruders?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.190
Well that’s where antigens come in.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:17.501
Antigens have a unique configuration that
our immune system is familiar with, when an

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:21.340
antibody attaches to a cell, it does so by
connecting with the cell’s antigens.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:26.550
If it recognizes the configuration of the
antigen it kindly buggers off and leaves it

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.760
in peace.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:33.361
If it can’t successfully attach to the antigen,
because it has a configuration that the antibody

00:03:33.361 --> 00:03:37.460
isn’t use to, it destroys the cell instead.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:43.270
It’s kind of like the antigen and the antibody
share a unique password, and only by exchanging

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.269
that password will the antibody leave that
cell alone.

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:52.460
There are two types of antigen, A and B and
the combination of those A and B antigens

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:57.190
that sits on your red blood cells, determines
your blood type.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:02.599
Someone who only has A antigens on their cells
is said to have an A blood type.

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:07.940
Similarly, having only B antigens means you
have a blood type of B. If you have a mix

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:14.120
of both A and B antigens on your cells you
have an AB blood type.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.989
People with an O blood type however, do not
have any A or B antigens, but they don’t

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:28.830
have O antigens either, because there’s
no such thing - they instead, have no antigens.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:33.139
Because O blood types don’t have antigens
on their cells, they are known as universal

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.139
donors.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:38.939
This means their blood can be given to anyone
with any blood type and it will be accepted

00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:41.210
by the recipient’s body.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:46.960
This is because an O-type blood cell doesn’t
have any antigens, so the recipient’s antibodies

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.080
don’t have any way to attach to the blood
cell and identify it.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:54.930
What normally happens when a patient receives
a blood transfusion is that the antibodies

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:01.110
are dispatched to latch onto the foreign blood
cells and detect whether it has A or B antigens

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.589
upon it.

00:05:02.589 --> 00:05:07.270
If the foreign antigens match the antigens
of the recipients own blood, then the body

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.119
accepts the new blood.

00:05:10.119 --> 00:05:15.089
For example, a recipient with a blood type
of A can only receive blood with A antigens

00:05:15.089 --> 00:05:18.639
or no antigens, as is the case with O-type
blood.

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:24.770
Similarly, B blood types can only receive
blood with B antigens or no antigens.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:30.479
People of an AB blood type however, are able
to receive blood with no antigens, A or B

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:36.360
antigens, or even a combination of both, because
their antibodies are setup to recognise and

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.060
accept both A and B antigens.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.930
AB blood types are therefore known as “universal
recipients” because they can receive any

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.439
blood type.

00:05:46.439 --> 00:05:49.990
If someone receives a blood transfusion of
the wrong blood type and their immune system

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:55.509
rejects it, for example, if an A blood type
receives B-type blood, then the results can

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:57.449
be catastrophic.

00:05:57.449 --> 00:06:01.479
If you are given the wrong blood type the
first thing you will experience is “a feeling

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:02.479
of impending doom”.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:06.219
Yes, that is actually a genuine, medical symptom.

00:06:06.219 --> 00:06:12.210
That feeling of impending doom will also be
accompanied by severe flu like symptoms.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.620
The cause of these symptoms is your immune
system attacking the foreign blood cells.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.889
Your body’s antibodies will detect that
the antigens of the foreign blood do not match

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:26.199
the type of antigens of your own blood cells,
and so it kills them.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:31.309
The attack which ensues upon the foreign blood
cells is both quick and aggressive.

00:06:31.309 --> 00:06:35.060
When foreign blood cells of a different blood
type are being killed off at such a rapid

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:40.569
rate, the dead blood cells can accumulate
in the veins and cause a blood clot.

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:46.919
This is known as an “acute hemolytic transfusion
reaction” and it can very quickly kill someone.

00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:52.610
Because of this, hospitals are extremely careful
and blood type mismatches happen very rarely,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:56.449
but when they do, the resulting fatality rate
is high.

00:06:56.449 --> 00:07:00.729
If you think that blood types seem rather
simple, I’m sorry to tell you, there’s

00:07:00.729 --> 00:07:03.479
a little more to it than I’ve been letting
on.

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:11.669
So up to now we have four blood types, A,
B, AB and O. However, there are actually eight

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:15.469
blood types, at least within the ABO system.

00:07:15.469 --> 00:07:24.409
Those are A+, A-, B+, B-, AB+, AB-, O+ and
O-.

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:27.319
So where do the positives and negatives come
from.

00:07:27.319 --> 00:07:31.569
It comes from a third type of antigen, called
the Rh factor.

00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:35.699
Named after rhesus monkeys, in which it was
first identified.

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:41.250
Your blood cells either have the Rh factor
antigens or they don’t.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:46.999
If the Rh factor antigens are present, then
you have a positive blood type, if they’re

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:50.169
not present you have a negative blood type.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:56.459
Rh negative blood may be given to patients
with either Rh negative or Rh positive blood

00:07:56.459 --> 00:08:03.219
types, providing their primary A, B or O blood
type matches, as we talked about previously.

00:08:03.219 --> 00:08:10.569
However Rh positive blood will only be accepted
by someone who also has Rh positive blood.

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:14.569
I know this complicates things somewhat, but
here’s a handy table that shows you which

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:18.710
donor blood types can be accepted by which
recipient blood types.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:22.779
So you can refer to it if you ever find yourself
having to give an emergency blood transfusion.

00:08:22.779 --> 00:08:24.120
You’re welcome.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:30.040
In some cases, if a mother with Rh negative
blood is carrying a foetus with Rh positive

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:34.919
blood, the mother’s antibodies can cross
the wall of the placenta and attack the unborn

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:35.930
baby.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.779
This is a condition known as “Haemolytic
disease of the newborn”.

00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:44.399
Some cultures believe that blood type actually
reflects an individual’s personality.

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:50.290
Just like some people use astrological signs
in the West, in Japan your ABO blood type

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:55.399
is believed to hold information about your
personality, temperament and compatibility

00:08:55.399 --> 00:08:56.589
with others.

00:08:56.589 --> 00:08:59.560
Although this is not supported by modern science.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:04.560
The belief is so strong, that in Japan, you’re
highly likely to be asked what blood type

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.680
you are when you interview for a new job,
or go on a date with a potential partner.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.220
If you’ve ever played a Japanese video game,
especially an RPG, you might have noticed

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.649
the character descriptions in the game’s
manual include the character’s blood type,

00:09:19.649 --> 00:09:23.670
right alongside features such as their name,
gender and height.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:27.899
You might think it strange that the game developers
have even thought about what blood type the

00:09:27.899 --> 00:09:29.339
game characters are.

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:34.889
But blood type-personality association is
so strong in Japanese culture, that it’s

00:09:34.889 --> 00:09:36.730
considered completely normal.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:41.330
Although it’s interesting to learn about,
there’s no need to keep a card in your wallet

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:45.540
or get a tattoo saying what blood type you
are, in case you’re in a near-fatal accident

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.910
and need to receive blood.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:52.589
Because these days, blood type tests are so
fast and efficient, that it’s quicker and

00:09:52.589 --> 00:09:56.980
more accurate for the doctors to run a test,
than to go rummaging through your wallet to

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:59.040
find out your blood type.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:05.779
The most common blood type is O+; O+ types
make up 40% of the population.

00:10:05.779 --> 00:10:13.920
Conversely, AB- is the rarest blood type,
only 1% of the world are AB-.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:20.529
Only 9% of the population have the precious
and always in need, O- blood type.

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:26.660
If you possess O- blood you should feel quite
empowered, because it’s the only blood type

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:32.420
that can be safely given to every human on
the planet.

